    "text_delta": "of It?\n\n**Legally and Professionally: No, unless you have concrete evidence.**\n\n* **High legal threshold:** To substantiate a claim of unlawful discrimination, you need more than panel demographics and a disappointment in the outcome. You would need evidence of procedural irregularities, discriminatory remarks, or a clear breach of municipal hiring policy.\n* **Reputational risk:** The municipal planning world is relatively small and interconnected. Alleging gender bias based purely on the panel's gender composition can harm your professional reputation among regional administrators, recruiters, and search firms.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up politely on your feedback request:** Send a brief, professional follow-up email to HR or the recruiter, framing your request strictly around personal and professional development:\n   > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Director position. I am following up on my request for feedback from the selection panel. As a dedicated municipal professional, any insights on areas where I can strengthen my candidacy for future executive roles would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n2. **Review public records access (if necessary):** In many jurisdictions, public sector hiring scoring matrices and interview notes can be requested through Freedom of Information (FOI/FOIA) or public records legislation. If you genuinely suspect procedural non-compliance or merit-rule violations, this is the formal mechanism to review the competition scoring before drawing conclusions.\n3. **Reflect on the interview performance:** Consider whether your interview answers emphasized technical competence over executive leadership, public engagement, and organizational strategy.\n",
